{{/* chezmoi:template:left-delimiter="[[#" right-delimiter="#]]" */}}
# Resolved by `op inject` at shell startup. Contains only references, never secrets.
#
# The line above retargets chezmoi's own template delimiters and is stripped from
# the rendered file. It has to be here: this source is a double-`.tmpl` so the
# destination keeps its `.tmpl` suffix for op-fast, which means chezmoi renders it
# as a Go template first — and the `{{ … }}` below is 1Password syntax, which Go
# would otherwise try to execute (`function "op" not defined`).
#
# Format is `NAME={{ reference }}` — no `export`, no quotes. ~/.config/op/load-env.zsh
# reads the injected output line by line and assigns with `typeset`; it does not
# eval it, because op-fast substitutes secret values as raw unquoted text, and a
# `"`, `$$` or `;` inside a value would otherwise be parsed as shell syntax. With
# no wrapper around the value there is nothing to strip, so nothing can be
# stripped wrongly — a secret that genuinely begins and ends with `"` keeps both.
#
# The braces are load-bearing, and so are the spaces inside them. op-fast's own
# scan for unenclosed references does not terminate them correctly here: a file
# with N unquoted `NAME=<scheme>...` lines fails the whole inject with
# `op inject returned 2(N-1) values for (N-1) references`. One such line injects
# fine, which is how this format looked correct until it was tried with all five.
# The enclosed form resolves every reference, including `Moonshot AI`, whose item
# name contains a space. Do not "simplify" the braces away.
TAVILY_API_KEY={{ op://Private/tavily-api/credential }}
TURBO_TOKEN={{ op://Private/turbo/token }}
TURBO_TEAM={{ op://Private/turbo/team }}
TURBO_REMOTE_CACHE_SIGNATURE_KEY={{ op://Private/turbo/signature }}
MOONSHOT_API_KEY={{ op://Private/Moonshot AI/credential }}

# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY is deliberately not exported at startup.
#
# NOTE: op-fast does NOT honour shell comments. It scans this file for secret
# references by scheme and resolves every one it finds, commented or not, so a
# commented-out example still gets fetched and cached on every inject. That is
# why the reference below is written with its scheme broken; restore the normal
# two-slash prefix when you type the command, and do not "tidy" this back into
# a literal URI — doing so silently re-adds the secret to the startup set.
#
#   export STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=$(op-fast read op:<slash><slash>Private/stripe-secret/credential)

# CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN is intentionally NOT loaded at startup: an exported token
# takes precedence over `wrangler login` OAuth. The cloudflare MCP servers OAuth
# separately (type:http, no env) and are unaffected. Load on demand only for
# non-interactive REST/CI use:
#   export CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=$(op-fast read op:<slash><slash>Private/cloudflare-api/credential)
